# ==============================================================================
# The mismatch analysis
# ==============================================================================

# The TinyLlama vocabulary is a SentencePiece *unigram* model, so the reference
# picks the segmentation with the best total score while the repository's
# SentencePiece path takes the greedy longest match. The scores come from the
# upstream GGUF, read with gguf-py:
$ python3 -c "... gguf.GGUFReader('stories260K.gguf') ..."
  reference (llama.cpp) '▁d' (-20) + 'on' (-30) + "'" (-180) + 't' (-154) = -384
  repository (greedy)  '▁do' (-141) + 'n' (-157) + "'" (-180) + 't' (-154) = -632

# The reference's segmentation is 248 log-probability units better, so the
# reference is right by the model's own scoring and the greedy longest match is
# the defect. The case is pinned as a strict xfail with the reason "7B/7C".
